+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md for motivation. The current `CategoryServiceImpl.delete` path deletes products that have no remaining category and uses a category lookup that can silently continue when the requested category is absent. Category associations are persisted through the product model, while carts and orders have their own product line data.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Make category deletion a classification-only operation for products.
+- Delete the full category subtree and clean product-category join rows safely.
+- Preserve transaction behavior and expose consistent not-found responses.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Changing product deletion semantics when a product is explicitly deleted.
+- Reworking cart or order persistence schemas.
+- Changing authorization rules for category administration.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Resolve and validate the requested category before calculating the subtree; missing or cross-store ids must raise the existing resource-not-found/authorization errors rather than become a no-op.
+- Load all subtree categories, remove each from every affected product's category set, and update the product only when associations changed. Never call product deletion from category deletion; products with no remaining categories are still valid retained products.
+- Delete categories after association cleanup, preserving the existing descendant ordering needed by the category repository.
+- Add regression coverage at the core service level and API integration level for subtree deletion, multi-category products, missing ids, and cart/order continuity. This keeps persistence behavior and HTTP mapping covered.
+
+Alternatives considered: soft-deleting categories would require changing every category query and would not satisfy the requirement that deleted ids return 404; deleting products is the existing behavior and directly causes the reported data loss.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Risk] Large category trees or product sets may require many entity updates. → Reuse existing subtree/product queries and keep the operation transactional; validate with representative integration coverage.
+- [Risk] Removing associations from managed/detached entities can leave stale in-memory relationships. → Reload products before mutation and verify API reads after deletion.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+No database migration is expected. Deploy the service change, then verify deletion against products with one and multiple categories and existing cart/order fixtures. Rollback is the normal application rollback; previously deleted products cannot be recovered by this change.
